red about the ba2 variant there is no  evidence that ba2 results in more severe  disease compared with the ba1 variant  nor does it appear to be more likely to  evade immune protection than ba1 but ba2  does appear to be more transmissible  than ba1  the high level of immunity in the  population from vaccines boosters and  previous infection will provide some  level of protection against va ii  however we strongly encourage everyone  to be up to date on their cova 19  vaccines  looking across the country we see that  95 percent of counties are reporting low  coven 19 community levels which  represents over 97 percent of the us  population  if we look more closely at the local  level we find a handful of counties  where we are seeing increases in both  cases and markers of more severe disease  like hospitalizations and inpatient bed  capacity which have resulted in an  increased cover 19 community level in  some areas  while on the national level cases remain  relatively low compared to prior points  in the pandemic we continue to look to  coven 19 community levels which were  developed to work locally at the county  level  as we move forward we encourage local  jurisdictions to closely monitor their  own covet 19 community levels and to  follow additional metrics that are  that they may have available as leading  indicators of disease for example  wastewater and syndromic surveillance  it's important for communities and  health officials to use these measures  to help inform prevention strategies for  their local area like masking testing  and plans for treatment should they move  into higher cover 19 community levels  now as we continue to closely monitor  kova 19 in the united states we're  reminded of the critical need to have a  sustainable and resilient public health  workforce in place that can effectively  respond to both emerging threats and  ongoing public health needs  during the decade prior to covet 19 the  public health workforce lost an  estimated 60 0